<\body>
  <doc-data|<doc-title|Peacok 15>>

  <\itemize-dot>
    <item>The overall properties of the universe are very close to being
    homogeneous; and yet telescopes reveal a wealth of detail on scales
    varying from single galaxies to large-scale structures of size exceeding
    100 Mpc

    <item>The existence of these cosmological structures tells us something
    important about the initial conditions of the big bang, and about the
    physical processes that have operated subsequently.

    <item>The aim of studying cosmological inhomogeneities is to understand
    the processes that caused the universe to depart from uniform density.

    <item>In studying how matter in the expanding universe responds to its
    own self-gravity, a linear solution for how the matter behaves can be
    found by expanding the equations of motion in terms of a dimensionless
    density perturbation <math|\<delta\>>:

    <\equation*>
      1+\<delta\><around*|(|<with|font-series|bold|x>|)>=<frac|\<rho\><around*|(|<with|font-series|bold|x>|)>|<around*|\<langle\>|\<rho\>|\<rangle\>>>
    </equation*>

    <item>The density content can be divided into nonrelativistic matter and
    radiation, and there are two distinct perturbation modes, with di\[erent
    relations between the two density components.
  </itemize-dot>

  <strong|Perturbaciones adiabaticas y perturbaciones isocurvas>

  <image|<tuple|<#>|png>|1par|||>

  El libro propone 2 tipos de perturbaciones, que tienen en cuenta a la
  materia y a la radiacion. En la perturbacion adiabatica, donde se perturba
  la densidad de forma adiabatica, las densidades de energia tanto para la
  materia y la radiacion cambian, siendo que:

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\><rsub|r>=4 \<delta\><rsub|m>/3
  </equation*>

  En la perturbacion isocurva en cambio, se perturba la densidad de entropia,
  pero no la densidad de energia. De manera que la densidad de energia total
  permanece igual. Aqui se cumple:

  <\equation*>
    \<rho\><rsub|r>\<delta\><rsub|r>=-\<rho\><rsub|m>\<delta\><rsub|m>
  </equation*>

  <\itemize-dot>
    <item>In general, modes of short wavelength have their amplitudes reduced
    relative to those of long wavelength in this way. Linear adiabatic
    perturbations scale with time as follows:

    <image|<tuple|<#>|png>|1par|||>

    <item>whereas isocurvature perturbations to the matter are initially
    constant, and then decline:

    <image|<tuple|<#>|png>|1par|||>
  </itemize-dot>

